## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to translate dependency constraints into a topological order and compute earliest finish times via DP. It also touches complexity and edge cases (cycles).

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort (Kahn or DFS)
- DAG longest path / critical path
- Dynamic programming on DAG
- Time and space complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function computeProject(durations, edges) {
  const n = durations.length;
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const est = Array(n).fill(0);
  const finish = Array(n).fill(0);
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let idx = 0;
  while (idx < q.length) {
    const u = q[idx++];
    finish[u] = est[u] + durations[u];
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      est[v] = Math.max(est[v], finish[u]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const project = Math.max(...finish);
  return { finish, project };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect cycles if the DAG assumption fails? 
- How would you adapt for tasks with minimum and maximum parallelism constraints?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to model task dependencies as a DAG and compute an optimal parallel schedule using topological ordering and dynamic programming.

## Key Concepts

- **Topological sort**: Ordering nodes such that all prerequisites come before dependent tasks
- **DAG DP**: Computing the longest path in a directed acyclic graph
- **Cycle detection**: Using indegree counts to identify cycles (if not all nodes are processed)
- **Time complexity**: O(n + m) where n is nodes and m is edges

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestFinish(n, edges, duration) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v] of edges) {
    adj[u].push(v);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const finish = duration.slice();
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  }

  let processed = 0;

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    processed++;

    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      finish[v] = Math.max(finish[v], finish[u] + duration[v]);
      indeg[v]--;
      if (indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }

  if (processed !== n) return -1;
  return Math.max(...finish);
}
```

## Key Takeaways

- Unlimited workers means tasks run in parallel when dependencies allow
- The finish time of a task is the maximum of (prerequisite finish + its own duration)
- The pipeline completes when the slowest path (critical path) finishes

## Why Asked
This question evaluates understanding of fundamental sorting algorithms and their practical trade-offs in real-world applications.

## Key Concepts
- Divide and conquer strategies
- Partitioning vs merging approaches
- In-place vs auxiliary space requirements
- Algorithm stability and performance characteristics

## Code Example
```
// Quicksort partition
function partition(arr, low, high) {
  const pivot = arr[high];
  let i = low - 1;
  for (let j = low; j < high; j++) {
    if (arr[j] < pivot) {
      i++;
      [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
    }
  }
  [arr[i + 1], arr[high]] = [arr[high], arr[i + 1]];
  return i + 1;
}

// Mergesort merge
function merge(left, right) {
  const result = [];
  let i = 0, j = 0;

  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i] <= right[j]) {
      result.push(left[i++]);
    } else {
      result.push(right[j++]);
    }
  }

  return result.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of robust sorting strategies used in production libraries. Combines quicksort behavior, 3-way partitioning for duplicates, and a guaranteed worst-case fallback with heapsort. Assesses pivot choice, in-place constraints, and complexity reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: hybrid of quicksort with depth-based fallback to heapsort
- 3-way partitioning: <, =, > pivot to handle duplicates efficiently
- Depth limit: d = floor(2*log2(n)) prevents O(n^2) degeneration
- In-place implementation with O(log n) recursion overhead

## Code Example

```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const depthLimit = n > 1 ? Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n)) : 0;

  function swap(i, j) {
    const t = arr[i]; arr[i] = arr[j]; arr[j] = t;
  }

  function heapSortSub(start, end) {
    const len = end - start;
    function heapify(i, heapSize) {
      let largest = i;
      while (true) {
        const l = 2 * i + 1;
        const r = l + 1;
        let idx = i;
        if (l < heapSize && arr[start + l] > arr[start + idx]) idx = l;
        if (r < heapSize && arr[start + r] > arr[start + idx]) idx = r;
        if (idx !== i) { [arr[start + i], arr[start + idx]] = [arr[start + idx], arr[start + i]]; i = idx; } else break;
      }
    }
    for (let i = Math.floor(len / 2) - 1; i >= 0; i--) heapify(i, len);
    for (let endIdx = len - 1; endIdx > 0; endIdx--) {
      swap(start, start + endIdx);
      heapify(0, endIdx);
    }
  }

  function quicksort3(start, end, depth) {
    if (end - start <= 1) return;
    if (depth <= 0) {
      heapSortSub(start, end);
      return;
    }
    const pivot = arr[start + Math.floor((start + end - 1) / 2)];
    let lt = start, i = start, gt = end - 1;
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (arr[i] < pivot) { swap(lt, i); lt++; i++; }
      else if (arr[i] > pivot) { swap(i, gt); gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    quicksort3(start, lt, depth - 1);
    quicksort3(gt + 1, end, depth - 1);
  }

  quicksort3(0, n, depthLimit);
}
```

